9. What Is CN?
======================================================================

CN means Common Name.

It represents the primary hostname associated with the certificate.

For our application:

CN=olympus.api.crmtoolympusnew.cloudgsl.nam.nsroot.net

======================================================================
10. What Are SAN Entries?
======================================================================

SAN means:

Subject Alternative Name

A certificate can be used for multiple DNS names.

For example, our service can be reached through:

Main/WIP hostname:
olympus.api.crmtoolympusnew.cloudgsl.nam.nsroot.net

PROD VIP:
vs-hdax-dxbg.nam.nsroot.net

COB VIP:
vs-bula-ifev.nam.nsroot.net

If those names need to present the same application certificate, they should be included as SANs as appropriate.

Example:

Subject Alternative Name:

DNS:
olympus.api.crmtoolympusnew.cloudgsl.nam.nsroot.net

DNS:
vs-hdax-dxbg.nam.nsroot.net

DNS:
vs-bula-ifev.nam.nsroot.net

======================================================================
11. Why Are SANs Important?
======================================================================

Suppose the certificate contains only:

olympus.api.crmtoolympusnew.cloudgsl.nam.nsroot.net

but we directly access:

https://vs-hdax-dxbg.nam.nsroot.net

If vs-hdax-dxbg.nam.nsroot.net is not covered by the certificate SANs, hostname verification can fail.

Therefore before requesting the certificate, identify all DNS names that must legitimately use that certificate.

Do not add random server names.

Only include the names required by the application architecture.

