33. Our Certificate Journey – What Happened
======================================================================

Now comes the lessons-learned section.

======================================================================
34. Attempt 1 – Downloaded Existing JKS
======================================================================

Initially, we followed an existing reference and downloaded a JKS from a Citi PKI/Confluence location.

We then attempted to make that JKS usable for CRM-to-Olympus by adding/generating application certificate/private-key related material using keytool commands.

Conceptually:

Downloaded generic JKS
       ↓
Added application certificate/key material
       ↓
Deployed to application

At the time, this appeared technically workable.

======================================================================
35. Issue With Attempt 1
======================================================================

We subsequently received communication that this was not the Citi-standard certificate creation approach for our application.

Major lesson:

A technically working keystore does not automatically mean it follows the approved enterprise certificate-management process.

Certificate identity and private-key generation should originate from the approved PKI/CMP process.

[ADD EMAIL SCREENSHOT SAYING THIS WAS NOT CITI STANDARD HERE]

======================================================================
36. Why Generating Our Own Key Material Was a Problem
======================================================================

The private key is part of the certificate identity.

Generating a private key independently and manually constructing a JKS introduces questions such as:

Who generated the key?

Was the key generated through the approved process?

Does the key match the approved certificate?

How is the key protected?

Can the certificate lifecycle be tracked?

Therefore the application certificate and private key should come from the approved CMP process.

======================================================================
37. Attempt 2 – Raised CMP
======================================================================

After receiving the feedback, we raised the proper CMP request.

The CMP process generated:

Certificate

Private Key

PFX

The PFX contained the certificate and private key together.

This corrected the certificate-generation source.

======================================================================
38. Attempt 2 – Imported CMP Material Into Existing Confluence JKS
======================================================================

At that stage, we still reused the JKS obtained from the earlier Confluence reference.

Conceptually:

CMP PFX
   ↓
Existing Confluence JKS
   ↓
Application JKS

This solved one problem:

Certificate/key now originated from CMP.

However, later we discovered another issue with the base JKS itself.

======================================================================
39. Issue With the Confluence JKS
======================================================================

We were informed that the JKS available through that Confluence/reference process was associated with Microsoft-provided PKI/trust material and should not be used for this application's long-term certificate solution.

That Microsoft-provided JKS approach was going to be decommissioned.

Therefore continuing to build our application keystore on top of that JKS was not appropriate.

Major lesson:

Do not use an unrelated generic keystore as the foundation for the application identity unless the platform/security standard specifically requires it.

[ADD EMAIL / COMMUNICATION ABOUT MICROSOFT JKS DECOMMISSIONING HERE]

======================================================================
40. Final Correct Approach
======================================================================

The CMP PFX already contained what the CRM-to-Olympus application needed:

Application certificate
+
Matching private key

Therefore there was no need for the old Confluence JKS.

We created a new standalone application JKS directly from the CMP PFX:

CMP
 ↓
CertAndKey.pfx
 ↓
keytool -importkeystore
 ↓
cacerts.jks
 ↓
Application deployment

This is the approach we now follow.

